---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import GuideNav from '../components/GuideNav.astro';
import PriceGauge from '../components/PriceGauge.astro';
import { loadFacets } from '../lib/facets';
import { priceBands } from '../lib/price';

const { facets, providers } = await loadFacets();
const entryPrice = facets.find((facet) => facet.id === 'entry-price');

/* Strings, not adjacent expressions: Astro drops the whitespace between two of those. */
const counts = priceBands.map((band) => {
  const count = entryPrice?.values.find((value) => value.id === band.id)?.count ?? 0;
  return { band, line: `${count} ${count === 1 ? 'provider starts here' : 'providers start here'}` };
});

const recorded = providers.filter((provider) => provider.priceFrom).length;
const ranged = providers.filter((provider) => provider.priceTo).length;
const coverage = `${recorded} of ${providers.length} records carry a starting band, and ${ranged} carry a range.`;

/* Examples chosen for shape, not for merit: each shows a different span. */
const examples = ['hetzner', 'aws', 'fortrabbit']
  .map((id) => providers.find((provider) => provider.id === id))
  .filter((provider) => provider?.priceFrom);
---

<Base
  title="How prices are recorded — FindHost"
  description="A seven-band scale in dollars a month, why it is a range rather than a figure, and what else about a bill is worth knowing."
>
  <p class="figure-band full-bleed" style="background:rgb(28,36,30);color:rgb(180,220,190)">
    <span aria-hidden="true">📐</span> A price you can compare, not a price we made up.
  </p>

  <div class="guide-layout full-width">
    <GuideNav current="/pricing/" />

    <article>
      <h1>How prices are recorded</h1>

      <p class="lead">
        Not a figure, and not a currency you may not use — a range across seven bands, in dollars a month, that says
        where a provider sits on the market rather than what your invoice will say.
      </p>

      <h2>The scale</h2>

      <PriceGauge from="xs" to="3xl" size="lg" labelled />

      <p>
        Each band is roughly three times the one before it. That is what puts the detail where the shopping happens: the
        first three bands cover everything under fifty dollars a month, which is where most of this market lives and
        where a linear scale would squash it all into one stripe. The last band runs to whatever a hyperscaler bill
        reaches, and the difference between two large numbers matters less than the difference between five dollars and
        fifteen.
      </p>

      <dl class="fact-list">
        {
          counts.map(({ band, line }) => (
            <>
              <dt>{band.label}</dt>
              <dd>{line}</dd>
            </>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h2>Why a range</h2>

      <p>
        A starting price answers one question — can I afford to begin — and then stops being useful. Shared hosting
        starts and finishes within a few pounds of itself. A hyperscaler starting at the same number has no ceiling
        worth naming. Two providers with an identical entry price can be nothing like each other, and the shape of the
        gauge is what shows it.
      </p>

      {
        examples.length > 0 && (
          <ul class="price-examples">
            {examples.map((provider) => (
              <li>
                <PriceGauge from={provider!.priceFrom} to={provider!.priceTo} size="md" />
                <a href={`/providers/${provider!.id}/`}>{provider!.name}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )
      }

      <p>
        The lower end is the cheapest way to run one small production application with every mandatory component
        included, at the <strong>standing</strong> price rather than an introductory one. Filtering for "under five dollars"
        and being handed a host that costs eleven after three months is the exact deception this dataset exists to correct
        — three records had their band corrected on exactly that during the last pass, and one lost its band altogether because
        the provider never publishes what the price becomes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The upper end depends on what is being sold. Where a provider publishes a ladder of plans, it is the top of that
        ladder. Where it meters resources with no ladder to end, it is where a serious deployment lands — never the
        largest invoice a provider could theoretically issue, which for a hyperscaler is a number with no meaning. Where
        neither can be established, there is no upper end recorded, and the gauge shows only where the provider starts.
      </p>

      <h2>Free is not a price</h2>

      <p>
        A free tier is not on this scale. It is recorded separately, as permanent, a trial, or none, because free
        describes what a provider gives away and the scale describes what it charges. A generous free tier attached to
        an expensive first paid plan is a common shape, and folding the two together would hide it.
      </p>

      <h2>The rest of the bill</h2>

      <p>
        Most unpleasant surprises are not in the headline number. They are in when it is charged and how hard it is to
        stop, so those are fields of their own:
      </p>

      <dl class="fact-list">
        <dt>Paid in advance</dt>
        <dd>Charged before the period it covers. The bill is known before it arrives, and unused capacity is spent.</dd>

        <dt>Paid in arrears</dt>
        <dd>
          Charged after the period, for what was used. The bill is not known until it lands, which is what makes a
          runaway job expensive.
        </dd>

        <dt>Billed</dt>
        <dd>Which periods are offered at all — hourly, daily, monthly, yearly, or a multi-year term.</dd>

        <dt>Getting out</dt>
        <dd>
          How long you can still be billed after deciding to leave, worst case. A notice period and a minimum term are
          the same thing to whoever is paying — time — so they add up rather than competing.
        </dd>

        <dt>Currencies</dt>
        <dd>What the provider will actually invoice in, which is not always the currency its pricing page displays.</dd>

        <dt>Renewal multiple</dt>
        <dd>
          The renewal price divided by the introductory one, so 3.3 means a €2.99 offer that renews at €9.99. A value
          below 1 would mean the price falls, which happens to nobody.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>Getting out is half of what a price costs</h2>

      <p>
        This used to be recorded as a cancellation policy, with values like "cancel any time" and "runs to the end of
        the paid term". Almost every host is both of those at once, which is how the phrase survives on so many pricing
        pages while telling you nothing. What a reader wants is a duration: from the moment you decide to leave, how
        long does the money keep going out?
      </p>

      <p>
        So that is what is recorded. A server billed by the hour stops costing when you stop it — a day. A monthly plan
        runs to the end of the month you have paid for. Thirty days' notice on a monthly plan is not a month, it is most
        of a quarter, because the notice and the remaining term are both owed. An annual term is a year, whatever the
        cancel button says.
      </p>

      <p>
        It sits beside the price on every record, because a cheap plan you cannot leave for a year is not a cheap plan.
      </p>

      <h2>What is recorded so far</h2>

      <p class="annotation">
        {coverage} An absent band means we have not established one, never that a provider is cheap or expensive. Where a
        provider publishes an introductory rate and a renewal rate that describe different products, no band is recorded at
        all, because picking either would mislead.
      </p>

      <p class="annotation">
        Nothing here is ordered by price, and no view sorts by it.
        <a href="/policies/">The governance policy</a> says why, and the
        <a href="/providers/">register</a> lets you filter by band without ever ranking on it.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
